Z||000000||DaveL||07-18-2000||03:51 PM||||I"ve posted a few questions about my auto trans shifting funny on odd occasions, with not too much solid advice. This is an 87 SE 2.5 DIS with about 80K. When checking the occasional code that keeps popping up, I've had code 24 several times. My Haynes book says to ignore this if set while the car isn't moving, so I did. Then it occurred to me that the code WAS set while moving. Could this be related to a shift point problem and/or speedo shutting off occasionally? I seem to be using the word "occasionally" more than occasionaly. Dang   Any ideas? How hard is the VSS to replace?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Gold-86SE||07-18-2000||05:04 PM||||<BR>Are you sure the connection on the speed sensor is achored properly and that there is not grit or grime interfering with the connection?<P>Just out of curiosity, when was the last time you serviced your trans fluid?<P><B>Replacement</B><BR>Replacement is easy, here is how.<P>If the speed sensor is like the one on my 86 4spd tranny, there is a 10mm bolt that tightens down a metal bar that holds the speed sensor in place.<P>Disconnect the sensor, remove the 10mm bolt, keep track of the metal bar, then gently pull up on the sensor itself, and it will come out of the tranny.<P>Put in a new speed sensor in reverse order. <P><B>Notes</B><BR>Just be sure that the metal bar fits snuggly in the groove on the sensor, otherwise you will leak oil. Also make sure that the new speed sensor has an 'O' ring, otherwise you will leak oil.<P>The location of the sensor, mine at least, is on top of the tranny. Stand behind the engine compartment, look down at the tranny, near the passenger side of the tranny, you will see a plug, that is the sensor.<P>If you replace the sensor, make sure you reset your computer.<P>------------------<BR>Gold-86SE (Fiero Collector)<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Gold-86SE (edited 07-18-2000).]||||reg||1
